P1: A timely veto's exact revert is abandoned after one workflow-dispatch failure following successful PR creation.

At warrant/gate.py:1380-1385, open_revert creates its PR and then dispatches its required workflows. A transient refusal of that dispatch returns FAILED, leaving the exact revert PR open. On every later run, _revert_only recognizes the real exact revert even though its required check is missing, so enforce_brakes includes its merge in pending (1555) and skips it (1561-1562). No later dispatch occurs. refresh_verdicts and approve_held both skip it because it is a revert, not verdicts-only (1400 onward, 1494). Thus the veto remains unenforced without intervention although the API recovered, no content conflict exists, and the veto stands.

This is the next partial-failure boundary after #554's repair: E102 fails creation of the PR after its branch exists; it does not fail dispatch after the PR exists. The code must safely resume the exact pending revert's missing CI, checking the assessed head and required workflows, rather than treating a pending PR as completion of every creation step. This is work for an ordinary constitutional-path pull request; no product change is made by this review.

The fixture creates real signed fixture records and real Git trees, commits and refs. Its API adapter injects one HTTP-502-style Refused exception from dispatch after the PR has been created. On the next three runs (before and after the brake ends), GT.assess finds the exact revert but refuses it only for CHECK_MISSING. enforce_brakes, refresh_verdicts and approve_held all return no action: total dispatch attempts stays one, approval attempts zero, CI never starts. With dispatch succeeding, the same scenario is merge-eligible on all three runs. The approval-required bot PR run is explicitly present in the failure fixture.

GitHub's current documentation states that GITHUB_TOKEN-created PR opened/synchronize/reopened events create approval-required runs, while workflow_dispatch starts runs directly:
https://docs.github.com/en/actions/concepts/security/github_token#when-github_token-triggers-workflow-runs
The fixture includes that approval-required state; it does not rely on the repository documentation's older assumption that opening the bot PR starts no run. No live API failure or real stalled revert is claimed.

